            <ArticleTitle>Prostaglandins and the mode of action of antipyretic analgesic drugs.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>19-23</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Antipyretic analgesics account for more than 95 percent of the analgesic market. The most important agents of this group--salicylates, aniline derivatives such as acetaminophen, and nonacidic pyrazolones--have been used for almost 100 years. Nevertheless, their therapeutic mechanisms and the molecular causes of the most important side effects remain unclear. Several common theories are discussed and challenged.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
